<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prithviraj Raso</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Prithviraj Raso is an epic poem in Brajbhasha attributed to Chand Bardai, said to have been the court poet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithviraj-chauhan">Prithviraj Chauhan</a>. It tells, with much legend, of Prithviraj's life, his elopement with Samyukta and his wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a>. The surviving versions date from the 16th century or later, but the work has deeply shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> historical memory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prithviraj-raso.jpg" alt="Prithviraj Raso" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: अजीत कुमार तिवारी · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prithviraj_Raso.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to tradition, Chand Bardai accompanied Prithviraj in all his battles, and the last canto, which tells of the deaths of poet and king, was composed by his son Jalha. Most modern scholars reject a date in Prithviraj's time: the language points much later, and the text treats the 13th-century King Samarsi as his contemporary. Narottamdas Swami, Namvar Singh and Cynthia Talbot date it to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, since two narratives of about 1587-1597, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a> and the Surjana-charita, summarise its plot. Rima Hooja and other scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> hold that its core was written in a language and style typical of the 12th and 13th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In the shortest recension Prithviraj, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> and grandson through his mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anangapala-ii">Anangpal Tomar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, is made king of Delhi. He refuses to take part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasuya">Rajasuya</a> ceremony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jaichand</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, whose daughter falls in love with him, and carries her off from her swayamvara, losing two-thirds of his hundred warriors. Infatuated with his wife, he neglects the threat from Shihab al-Din Muhammad Ghuri, who defeats him, takes him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and has him blinded. During an archery display arranged by Chand Bardai, Prithviraj kills the sultan by shooting towards his voice, and poet and king then kill each other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The text grew greatly through interpolations under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> patrons and now exists in four recensions, the longest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> manuscript, running to 16,306 stanzas; Cynthia Talbot listed nearly 170 manuscripts. A Mewar version compiled by the poet Karuna-udadhi for Amar Singh II, generally dated 1703, enlarged the part of his ancestor Samar Singh, who in fact lived nearly a century after Prithviraj.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> rulers valued the work for its heroic exploits, romance and revenge, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-tod">James Tod</a> introduced it to Western scholarship as an authentic historical source. Kaviraj Shyamaldas questioned its chronology in 1886, and later research showed many errors: Prithviraj's mother was a Kalachuri princess, not a Tomara, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> ruled for more than a decade after Prithviraj's death. It remains a source on the social and clan structure of the Kshattriya communities of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prithviraj Raso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prithviraj became a hero of Rajput legend through the <b>Prithviraj Raso</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/chahamanas-of-shakambhari">Chahamanas of Shakambhari</a></li></ul></section>
